# Copy to $NOVAK_HOME/.env (up.sh does this for you on first run).
#
# ── HOW TO READ THIS FILE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   EDIT-ME          You must replace this. up.sh lists any that remain.
#   set-in-keychain  A SECRET. Leave this line alone — put the real value in
#                    the macOS Keychain, where it cannot be read out of a file
#                    or committed by accident:
#                        security add-generic-password -s "novak/<VAR>" -a novak -w
#                    up.sh loads Keychain items and they override this file.
#                    Do so AS THE ACCOUNT THAT RUNS THE STACK — the login
#                    keychain is per-user.
#   anything else    A sensible default. Change only if you need to.
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  1. MUST EDIT — nothing works properly until these are real
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Hostname or IP other machines use to reach this one. A Tailscale name or
# address is a good choice; `localhost` will not do.
#
# THIS DOES NOT CHANGE WHAT ANYTHING BINDS TO. It is only used to build the
# URLs handed to browsers and to Home Assistant. If a service is unreachable
# from another machine, the cause is its bind address, not this setting —
# run `novak ports` to see which. oMLX in particular binds per its own
# admin-UI setting and defaults to localhost only.
HOST_NAME=EDIT-ME

# Vikunja's API root. Must end in /api/v1.
VIKUNJA_URL=EDIT-ME

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  2. MUST EDIT — only if you are running the optional web console
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Skip these if you are not using Konzol; the stack runs without it.

# Your Pocket ID (or other OIDC provider) base URL, no trailing slash.
OIDC_ISSUER=https://inti.a64.one
# The client ID you registered there. NOT a secret — client IDs are public,
# which is why this one is edited here rather than kept in the Keychain.
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=EDIT-ME

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  3. SECRETS — leave these lines exactly as they are
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Every value below comes from the Keychain. The literal text below is never
# used; it exists so compose has something to interpolate and so you can see
# at a glance what the stack expects.

# oMLX API key (enable auth in oMLX, then store the key).
OMLX_API_KEY=set-in-keychain

# Hindsight. WITHOUT THIS THE MEMORY ENDPOINT IS OPEN — no authentication,
# every bank readable by anything that can reach the port.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
HINDSIGHT_API_KEY=set-in-keychain

# Console session signing key.  openssl rand -base64 32
CONSOLE_AUTH_SECRET=set-in-keychain
# OIDC client secret (this one IS secret, unlike the client ID above).
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=set-in-keychain

# Open WebUI's session signing key. WITHOUT THIS, every restart or recreate
# of the container generates a new random one and logs everyone out.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
WEBUI_SECRET_KEY=set-in-keychain

# Per-endpoint tokens for external MCP servers. One per instance — the
# registry entry names which key belongs to which URL via its `auth:` field.
OUTLINE_EVERYTHING_API_KEY=set-in-keychain
TUDUDI_API_TOKEN=set-in-keychain
VIKUNJA_API_TOKEN=set-in-keychain

# The brave-search registry entry (a `container` entry, unlike the three
# above) passes this straight into that container as BRAVE_API_KEY. Get a
# key at https://api.search.brave.com/ — the free tier (2,000 searches/mo)
# is the plan this was evaluated against.
BRAVE_API_KEY=set-in-keychain

# The open-terminal registry entry ships disabled — see registry/mcp-servers.yaml
# for what it actually grants before turning it on. Generated key, not an
# account credential:  openssl rand -hex 32
OPEN_TERMINAL_API_KEY=set-in-keychain

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  4. DEFAULTS — fine as they are
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Ports. Change only on a collision.
# 8000 is oMLX's own default, confirmed against a running instance — this
# was 8080 here from the start, which is how a previous setup ended up with
# Open WebUI and Hindsight both silently pointed at a dead port (STATE.md,
# 2026-08-19). Noticed again while wiring the router to the real port.
#
# This is ONLY for novak ports' own reachability check (is oMLX listening
# on THIS host, on this port) — it no longer builds any other service's
# URL. See DEFAULT_ENGINE_BASE_URL below for that.
OMLX_PORT=8000

# The default engine every service reaches unless it has its own more
# specific setting: Open WebUI, Hindsight, and the router (which gets the
# whole .env passed through, so this needs no extra wiring there) all
# fall back to this. A REAL, COMPLETE URL, set directly here — not built
# from a host.docker.internal assumption baked into docker-compose.yml
# (decision #28/#30). host.docker.internal needs Docker's host-gateway
# extra_hosts entry to work at all outside macOS — present here, verified
# working on Linux/Unraid too — but the point of making this a plain .env
# value is that changing where the engine lives is one edit here, not a
# hunt through compose interpolation to find every place that assumed
# the same host.
#
# The default below is right for the common single-host case (oMLX or
# Ollama on the same machine as everything else). Point it at a router or
# a remote engine instead once that's not true — see docs/engines.md.
DEFAULT_ENGINE_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8000/v1

# Which model Hindsight uses to extract memories. Must be a model oMLX actually
# serves — its own default (gpt-4o-mini) does not exist locally and extraction
# fails silently apart from a line in the logs.
HINDSIGHT_LLM_MODEL=Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-4bit

# Override for when Hindsight should reach something OTHER than
# DEFAULT_ENGINE_BASE_URL above — most often the router's `task` model instead of
# an engine directly, since extraction is exactly the fast/
# no-persona-needed shape `task` exists for. Blank (the default) means
# Hindsight uses DEFAULT_ENGINE_BASE_URL, same as everything else.
#   HINDSIGHT_LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000/v1
#   HINDSIGHT_LLM_MODEL=task
HINDSIGHT_LLM_BASE_URL=

# ── Public addresses (set these when a reverse proxy is in front) ─────────
# HOST_NAME is how machines in this stack reach each other. It is NOT how a
# person reaches a service when a proxy fronts it — that name lives on another
# host, often a public domain, and nothing here can discover it.
#
# Leave blank and services build their URLs from HOST_NAME, which is correct
# for direct access. Set them to the address users actually type, scheme and
# all, with no trailing slash:
#   OWUI_PUBLIC_URL=https://cas.example.tld
#
# Getting this wrong is rarely a hard error. It surfaces as a login that
# bounces you to a hostname you never asked for, or a link in the UI that
# points somewhere unreachable.
OWUI_PUBLIC_URL=
KONZOL_PUBLIC_URL=

# Open WebUI: let its database override these settings. Default false, so .env
# wins and configuration cannot drift out of sight inside a container. Set true
# only if you want to manage Open WebUI from its admin UI instead — and then
# expect changes here to be ignored once its DB has a value.
OWUI_PERSISTENT_CONFIG=false

# ── Open WebUI sign-in via Pocket ID (optional) ───────────────────────────
# Unset means local accounts only, which is a fine place to start. Open WebUI
# reads these at startup and has no admin UI for them, so they belong here.
#
# Needs its OWN Pocket ID client, separate from the console's — the redirect
# URI differs and one client cannot serve both:
#   http://<HOST_NAME>:3000/oauth/oidc/callback
#
# OWUI_OIDC_ISSUER is the discovery document, not the bare issuer:
#   https://<pocket-id-host>/.well-known/openid-configuration
OWUI_OIDC_ISSUER=
OWUI_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OWUI_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=set-in-keychain
OWUI_OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=Pocket ID
# Leave blank. It follows OWUI_PUBLIC_URL (or HOST_NAME when that is unset),
# which is what you want — set OWUI_PUBLIC_URL instead. Override only if the
# callback must differ from the site's own address.
#
# Whatever it resolves to must be registered with the IdP EXACTLY, including
# scheme and path. Check the effective value with:
#   docker compose config | grep OPENID_REDIRECT_URI
OWUI_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=
# true lets anyone your IdP authenticates create an account here.
OWUI_OIDC_SIGNUP=false

# admins.novak already gates the console (deploy-checklist.md) — reusing it
# here means one Pocket ID change grants admin in both surfaces instead of
# maintaining two groups. Role and group management are separate switches in
# Open WebUI reading the same `groups` claim: role management grants the
# admin ROLE, group management syncs an actual Open WebUI GROUP, which is
# what "Restricted to admins.novak" on a model checks. An unmatched claim
# grants neither, so a wrong claim path is a silent no-op, not an accidental
# admin grant — VERIFY the exact claim shape by decoding the ID token after
# the first Pocket ID login before relying on this.
OWUI_OIDC_ROLE_MANAGEMENT=true
OWUI_OIDC_ROLES_CLAIM=groups
OWUI_OIDC_ADMIN_ROLES=admins.novak
OWUI_OIDC_GROUP_MANAGEMENT=true
OWUI_OIDC_GROUP_CLAIM=groups

# ── Home Assistant MCP (optional; that registry entry ships disabled) ─────
# The ha-mcp entry declares these. It is a `container` entry, so both the URL
# and the token are variables here — unlike `external` entries, which carry
# their URL in registry/mcp-servers.yaml and need only a token.
HA_MCP_URL=
HA_MCP_TOKEN=set-in-keychain
# A clustered block (13400-13409), not scattered across whatever each
# upstream project happened to default to. Changed after a real collision
# (decision #31): 8888 is Hindsight's old default and also a genuinely
# common default elsewhere — this exact box already had something else on
# it. A high, uncommon, contiguous range is far less likely to collide
# with anything else you already run, and is one thing to firewall/
# document instead of ten scattered numbers. Only affects fresh
# deployments — an existing one's real .env already has its own explicit
# values and is untouched by this file changing.
OPENWEBUI_PORT=13400
CONSOLE_PORT=13401
# 13402 is ROUTER_PORT, set further down where the router itself is —
# kept in this block for the numbering, not moved, so the two don't
# silently drift apart.
HINDSIGHT_PORT=13403
HINDSIGHT_UI_PORT=13404
# 13405/13406 (whisper/piper) retired, decision #39 — Home Assistant's own
# native Whisper/Piper add-ons do this now; see docs/home-assistant.md.
OPENWAKEWORD_PORT=13407

# Hindsight's inference provider. `openai` plus a local base URL points it at
# oMLX — the setting that keeps memory extraction on this machine.
HINDSIGHT_LLM_PROVIDER=openai

# Wake word. openwakeword CRASH-LOOPS until a model with this name exists in
# $NOVAK_HOME/wakeword/models/ — set a stock word (e.g. ok_nabu) until you have
# trained one. See wakeword/README.md.
WAKEWORD_MODEL=hey_novak

# ── Portal (single glass pane over Open WebUI and Konzol, optional) ───────
# Novak's first reverse proxy — Caddy fronting a static tabs page, gated by
# TinyAuth against Pocket ID. It does not front Open WebUI or Konzol
# themselves; they keep their own ports and their own Pocket ID logins.
# See docs/proxy.md for the full design and what was actually verified.
#
# MUST be a real URL with scheme, not a bare hostname and never an IP —
# confirmed directly: TinyAuth refuses to start against either. mini.local
# or a Tailscale MagicDNS name both work.
PORTAL_APPURL=EDIT-ME
PORTAL_PORT=13408
TINYAUTH_PORT=13409

# TinyAuth wants each OIDC endpoint spelled out — it does not read a
# discovery document the way Open WebUI and the console's OIDC libraries
# do. Copy these three verbatim from Pocket ID's own
# /.well-known/openid-configuration rather than guessing the path shape.
TINYAUTH_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=EDIT-ME
TINYAUTH_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=set-in-keychain
TINYAUTH_OIDC_AUTH_URL=EDIT-ME
TINYAUTH_OIDC_TOKEN_URL=EDIT-ME
TINYAUTH_OIDC_USERINFO_URL=EDIT-ME

# admins.novak already gates the console and Open WebUI's admin role
# (docker-compose.yml's OWUI_OIDC_* block) — reused here rather than a
# fourth group. VERIFY this restriction end to end with an account NOT in
# the group before trusting it: the OIDC connection and TINYAUTH_APPURL
# validation were tested directly against a running container, the actual
# group-match behaviour against a live Pocket ID login was not.
TINYAUTH_ADMIN_GROUP=admins.novak

# ── Inference router (decision #21, optional) ─────────────────────────────
# Injects Novak's persona once, in router/persona_hook.py, instead of a
# copy pasted into every client's own model config. Needs only
# OMLX_API_KEY, already set above if oMLX's own auth is on — which
# security.md's Rule 4 already asks for regardless of this. No client
# reconfiguration beyond pointing its base URL at this port instead of
# oMLX's: the model names (chat, deep, ha-voice) are unchanged.
ROUTER_PORT=13402

# Override for when Open WebUI should reach something OTHER than
# DEFAULT_ENGINE_BASE_URL above — most often the router, to get the persona
# injected (decision #21/#23). Blank (the default) means Open WebUI uses
# DEFAULT_ENGINE_BASE_URL directly, same as everything else — unchanged behavior,
# nothing switches over on its own. Set to a full URL, scheme and port,
# e.g. the router on this same host:
#   OWUI_INFERENCE_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1
# or a router on a different one entirely (decision #28):
#   OWUI_INFERENCE_BASE_URL=http://mitochon.tailnet-name.ts.net:4000/v1
# The model names (chat, deep, ha-voice) are unchanged either way.
#
# OWUI_INFERENCE_PORT (a bare port number) used to be how this worked,
# built into a host.docker.internal address inside docker-compose.yml.
# Removed (decision #28): host.docker.internal can only ever reach THIS
# machine, so a port-only setting was structurally unable to express "the
# router is on a different host" — and burying that construction inside
# compose interpolation meant changing engines meant hunting through
# compose files, not editing one line here. A plain full URL does both:
# works for local or remote, and is the whole answer, visible in .env.
OWUI_INFERENCE_BASE_URL=

# Points Open WebUI's background calls (title, tags, follow-ups,
# autocomplete) at the router's `task` model instead of whatever it's
# actively chatting with. Only meaningful once OWUI_INFERENCE_BASE_URL
# above points at the router — `task` isn't a model name anywhere else.
# Set to task once it does; measured directly, this is the difference between
# 15 and 574 prompt tokens for the same trivial request (persona
# injection on `chat`/`deep` vs. none on `task`). Still has to be set
# again in Open WebUI's own Admin Settings -> Interface -> Task Model
# (both Local and External) the first time — this line only makes that
# choice survive a restart, not makes it for you.
OWUI_TASK_MODEL=

# A second (or third, ...) engine (decision #28, docs/engines.md) is NOT
# a fixed setting here — that was tried and removed. registry/engines.yaml
# lets each engine declare its OWN base_url_var, any name you choose, so
# a second engine means adding an entry there and a matching line here
# with whatever name you gave it, e.g.:
#   OLLAMA_MYHOST_BASE_URL=http://myhost.tailnet-name.ts.net:11434/v1
# Regenerate with `novak router apply` after editing registry/engines.yaml
# — router/config.yaml is generated from it, never edited by hand.

# ── Ollama (Linux/GPU engine, decision #28/#33, optional) ─────────────────
# Only relevant once the `ollama` profile is enabled (docker-compose.yml) —
# irrelevant, and safely ignored, on a host running oMLX instead. See
# docs/ollama-settings.md for real numbers measured on Spire (RDNA4/Vulkan).
OLLAMA_PORT=11434
# Vulkan beat ROCm outright on RDNA4 in real testing — no HSA_OVERRIDE
# hacks needed. Set OLLAMA_VULKAN=0 for a ROCm-only card.
OLLAMA_VULKAN=1
OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=2
OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=4
OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=20m

# ── Data directory overrides (decision #33, optional) ─────────────────────
# Every service below defaults to a Docker-managed named volume — fine for
# a single-disk deployment. Set any of these to a real host path instead to
# bind-mount that service's data onto other storage (e.g. Spire's
# /mnt/teracache split: compose manifests + .env stay in appdata, but
# databases/media/models live on bulk storage). Blank means "keep the named
# volume", so this is a zero-risk no-op on a deployment that never sets it.
OWUI_DATA_DIR=
HINDSIGHT_DATA_DIR=
OLLAMA_DATA_DIR=
